    "id": "prop:second-order-risk-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Define the deletion contrast and the action-loss contrast by\n\\[\nD_{n,m}(\\beta)\n=d_{T_n^{\\mathrm{full}}}(\\beta)\n-\\left(\\frac Nn\\right)^2\nE_{S_m}d_{T_{n,m}^{\\mathrm{main}}}(\\beta)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nL_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta)\n=\\left(\\frac Nn\\right)^2\nE\\!\\left[\nV_{T_{n,m}^{\\mathrm{main}}}(\\delta_m,\\beta)\n-V_{T_{n,m}^{\\mathrm{main}}}\n\\{G_{T_{n,m}^{\\mathrm{main}}}(\\beta),\\beta\\}\n\\right].\n\\]\nThen, for every pilot-measurable action,\n\\[\nR_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta)-R_n^{\\mathrm{or}}(\\beta)\n=D_{n,m}(\\beta)+L_{n,m}(\\delta_m,\\beta).\n\\]\nThe quantity \\(L_{n,m}\\) is a contrast and need not be nonnegative because the empirical Gaussian oracle is not assumed to minimize exact finite Horvitz--Thompson risk.  If \\(s_{n,m}=m/n^2+1/(nm)\\), the proposed second-order expansion for \\(\\widehat G_m\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal K}\n\\frac{\\left|\n\\{D_{n,m}(\\beta)-A(\\beta)m/n^2\\}\n+\\{L_{n,m}(\\widehat G_m,\\beta)-B(\\beta)/(nm)\\}\n\\right|}{s_{n,m}}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nSeparate little-oh bounds for the two braced terms are sufficient but, without a no-cancellation premise, are not necessary.  Every proposed arbitrary-action lower bound is likewise exactly equivalent to the result obtained by substituting \\(D_{n,m}+L_{n,m}\\); there is no third cross-wave term.",
